WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.420
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.920
Rub a balloon and it can attract tiny pieces of paper.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:09.200
Something invisible has changed, but we haven't created a new kind of matter.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:11.500
We have transferred electric charge.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:13.860
There are positive and negative charges.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:19.280
A neutral body contains both, with equal total amounts, so its net charge is

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:19.560
zero.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:24.160
In ordinary rubbing and charging of solids, electrons are transferred between

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:29.020
materials; the positively charged atomic nuclei do not usually travel from one

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:29.680
object to another.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:33.740
If a body loses electrons, its net charge becomes positive.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:36.900
If it gains electrons, it becomes negative.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.320
The total charge of an isolated system is conserved.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:45.420
Charge adds algebraically, so plus three microcoulombs and minus five

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.520
microcoulombs give minus two microcoulombs overall.

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:51.480
A microcoulomb is one millionth of a coulomb.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:56.080
Ordinary isolated body charge comes in integer multiples of the elementary

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:00.640
charge, approximately one point six times ten to the minus nineteen coulombs.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:05.260
If a body gains ten trillion electrons, its charge change is minus one point

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:06.480
six microcoulombs.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:08.260
Don't lose that minus sign.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:13.180
Attraction of neutral paper doesn't prove that the paper had an opposite net

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:13.960
charge beforehand.

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:17.160
Its charges can rearrange, creating polarization.

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:22.180
Conductors allow mobile charges to redistribute; insulators restrict their

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:22.480
motion.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:27.700
For JEE, separate charge transfer, net charge and polarization before deciding

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:29.340
what an observation really tells you.

00:01:29.664 --> 00:01:32.384
Let's put two stationary point charges in vacuum.

00:01:33.204 --> 00:01:36.684
Coulomb's law says the force magnitude is proportional to the product of their

00:01:36.684 --> 00:01:40.244
charge magnitudes and inversely proportional to the square of their separation.

00:01:41.224 --> 00:01:44.104
The constant is approximately nine billion in SI units.

00:01:44.944 --> 00:01:48.704
The line joining the charges gives the force direction: like signs repel,

00:01:49.324 --> 00:01:50.124
opposite signs attract.

00:01:51.124 --> 00:01:55.044
Use the absolute charge product for magnitude, then decide direction from the

00:01:55.044 --> 00:01:55.804
signs and geometry.

00:01:55.804 --> 00:02:00.384
Suppose plus two microcoulombs and minus three microcoulombs are thirty

00:02:00.384 --> 00:02:01.444
centimeters apart.

00:02:02.224 --> 00:02:05.584
Convert the charges to coulombs and the distance to zero point three meters.

00:02:06.624 --> 00:02:10.584
The force magnitude is nine times ten to the ninth, times six times ten to the

00:02:10.584 --> 00:02:13.824
minus twelve, divided by zero point zero nine.

00:02:13.824 --> 00:02:15.304
That is zero point six newtons.

00:02:15.864 --> 00:02:18.584
On each charge, the force points toward the other.

00:02:19.224 --> 00:02:23.184
The forces have equal magnitudes and opposite directions, even if the charges

00:02:23.184 --> 00:02:23.964
or masses differ.

00:02:23.964 --> 00:02:26.744
Equal force does not require equal acceleration.

00:02:27.424 --> 00:02:28.324
Now double the separation.

00:02:28.844 --> 00:02:29.284
What happens?

00:02:29.724 --> 00:02:30.524
Give yourself a moment.

00:02:31.204 --> 00:02:35.191
The denominator becomes four times larger, so the force becomes zero point one

00:02:35.191 --> 00:02:35.784
five newtons.

00:02:36.584 --> 00:02:38.504
These are electrostatic point charge results.

00:02:39.404 --> 00:02:43.024
Nearby extended charged conductors can redistribute charge, so don't blindly

00:02:43.024 --> 00:02:46.024
treat every object's entire charge as concentrated at its center.

00:02:46.864 --> 00:02:50.644
A uniform dielectric can change the interaction constant under the appropriate

00:02:50.644 --> 00:02:53.224
ideal model; our worked example uses vacuum.

00:02:53.472 --> 00:02:54.872
Now add a third charge.

00:02:55.432 --> 00:02:57.092
Do we invent a new three charge formula?

00:02:57.732 --> 00:02:58.052
No.

00:02:58.572 --> 00:03:02.372
For point charges at specified positions, calculate the force from each other

00:03:02.372 --> 00:03:04.972
charge independently, then add the force vectors.

00:03:05.552 --> 00:03:06.372
That's superposition.

00:03:07.092 --> 00:03:08.872
Always choose the target charge first.

00:03:09.432 --> 00:03:13.072
Otherwise it's surprisingly easy to add forces acting on different objects,

00:03:13.512 --> 00:03:15.312
which is not a valid net force calculation.

00:03:16.052 --> 00:03:19.032
Let's place a positive one microcoulomb target at the origin.

00:03:19.472 --> 00:03:24.172
A positive one microcoulomb source sits thirty centimeters to its right, and

00:03:24.172 --> 00:03:27.452
another equal positive source sits thirty centimeters above it.

00:03:27.972 --> 00:03:30.072
The right hand source pushes the target left.

00:03:30.472 --> 00:03:32.192
The upper source pushes it downward.

00:03:32.952 --> 00:03:35.332
Each force magnitude is zero point one newtons.

00:03:36.032 --> 00:03:40.712
Therefore the x component is minus zero point one and the y component is minus

00:03:40.712 --> 00:03:41.772
zero point one newtons.

00:03:42.192 --> 00:03:46.292
The resultant magnitude is the square root of their squared sum, about zero

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:50.052
point one four one newtons, directed diagonally down and left.

00:03:50.992 --> 00:03:54.732
Adding the two magnitudes to get zero point two would be wrong because the

00:03:54.732 --> 00:03:55.612
forces are perpendicular.

00:03:56.512 --> 00:04:00.452
If instead equal positive sources sit symmetrically on the left and right,

00:04:00.912 --> 00:04:02.412
their forces cancel at the center.

00:04:03.172 --> 00:04:05.012
Each individual force is still present.

00:04:05.692 --> 00:04:09.932
With unequal sources, write the signed component equation and solve; don't

00:04:09.932 --> 00:04:11.912
assume cancellation happens at the midpoint.

00:04:12.592 --> 00:04:17.632
In JEE, a small direction sketch before substitution can save a perfectly good

00:04:17.632 --> 00:04:20.012
numerical calculation from a wrong final answer.

00:04:20.376 --> 00:04:24.696
Electric field lets us describe the influence of source charges before choosing

00:04:24.696 --> 00:04:26.116
the charge that will feel it.

00:04:26.356 --> 00:04:30.496
At a point, the field is force per unit positive test charge, with the test

00:04:30.496 --> 00:04:33.096
charge small enough not to disturb the source arrangement.

00:04:34.276 --> 00:04:35.916
Its unit is newtons per coulomb.

00:04:36.736 --> 00:04:40.476
For a point source, field magnitude is the Coulomb constant times the source

00:04:40.476 --> 00:04:42.856
charge magnitude divided by distance squared.

00:04:44.016 --> 00:04:47.236
Field points away from a positive source and toward a negative source.

00:04:47.956 --> 00:04:52.056
Notice that the field belongs to the source arrangement, not to the sign of the

00:04:52.056 --> 00:04:53.176
charge we later place there.

00:04:53.996 --> 00:04:58.016
A positive charge feels force along the field; a negative charge feels force

00:04:58.016 --> 00:04:58.736
opposite to it.

00:04:59.736 --> 00:05:03.836
At twenty centimeters from a positive two microcoulomb source in vacuum, field

00:05:03.836 --> 00:05:07.476
magnitude is four hundred and fifty thousand newtons per coulomb, directed

00:05:07.476 --> 00:05:07.876
outward.

00:05:09.116 --> 00:05:11.256
Place a negative one nanocoulomb test charge there.

00:05:11.556 --> 00:05:16.156
Its force magnitude is zero point zero zero zero four five newtons and its

00:05:16.156 --> 00:05:17.116
direction is inward.

00:05:18.176 --> 00:05:20.676
Field lines indicate the local field direction by their tangent.

00:05:21.736 --> 00:05:25.216
They do not cross where a unique nonzero field exists, and they are not

00:05:25.216 --> 00:05:27.256
automatically the paths moving particles follow.

00:05:28.216 --> 00:05:32.416
To combine several sources, add field vectors first, then multiply by the

00:05:32.416 --> 00:05:33.256
chosen test charge.

00:05:34.176 --> 00:05:38.036
Two equal opposite charges can produce a strong field between them even though

00:05:38.036 --> 00:05:39.356
their total net charge is zero.

00:05:40.136 --> 00:05:43.176
Total charge alone doesn't determine the field at every point.

00:05:43.296 --> 00:05:46.876
Take equal positive and negative charges separated by distance d.

00:05:47.936 --> 00:05:49.476
Together they form an electric dipole.

00:05:50.416 --> 00:05:55.076
Its dipole moment has magnitude q times the full separation, and its direction

00:05:55.076 --> 00:05:57.776
is from the negative charge to the positive charge.

00:05:58.436 --> 00:06:02.736
In a uniform external field, the positive charge feels a force along the field

00:06:03.216 --> 00:06:06.096
while the negative charge feels an equal force opposite to it.

00:06:06.876 --> 00:06:11.856
Net force is zero, but if the dipole is tilted these forces act along different

00:06:11.856 --> 00:06:12.076
lines.

00:06:12.896 --> 00:06:14.016
They create a torque.

00:06:14.596 --> 00:06:18.596
Its magnitude is dipole moment times field times sine of the angle between

00:06:18.596 --> 00:06:21.276
them, and the vector form is p cross E.

00:06:22.036 --> 00:06:24.336
The torque tends to align the dipole with the field.

00:06:25.156 --> 00:06:29.416
For charges of two nanocoulombs separated by one centimeter, dipole moment is

00:06:29.416 --> 00:06:31.756
two times ten to the minus eleven coulomb meters.

00:06:32.536 --> 00:06:36.196
In a uniform field of one hundred thousand newtons per coulomb at thirty

00:06:36.196 --> 00:06:39.936
degrees, torque is one times ten to the minus six newton meters.

00:06:40.856 --> 00:06:45.796
Both aligned and opposite alignment give zero torque, but aligned orientation

00:06:45.796 --> 00:06:48.456
is stable and opposite alignment is unstable.

00:06:49.696 --> 00:06:51.456
Zero torque alone doesn't prove stability.

00:06:52.616 --> 00:06:56.476
Far from the dipole compared with its separation, its axial field magnitude is

00:06:56.476 --> 00:07:01.686
approximately twice k p divided by r cubed, while the equatorial magnitude is k

00:07:01.686 --> 00:07:04.616
p divided by r cubed and points opposite to p.

00:07:06.056 --> 00:07:09.756
Those are far field approximations, not exact formulas close to a charge.

00:07:10.616 --> 00:07:13.756
A nonuniform external field can also give a dipole a net force.

00:07:14.996 --> 00:07:18.096
Next we'll build on these field ideas with flux and Gauss's law.
